# OCR audit: the OCR cache is derived from image bytes and is regenerable, and it
# can reach tens of MB. The reports themselves ARE committed; the cache is not.
# OCR audit derived artefacts.
#
# The readable answers (every .md report, provenance.json, search_index.sqlite)
# ARE committed. Two large binary products are not, because they are fully
# regenerable from the same command and would otherwise add ~64 MB to every
# clone:
#
#   evidence.jsonl    ~26 MB  one record per image
#   contact_sheets/   ~38 MB  130 rendered image grids
#
# They stay on disk after a run. `provenance.json` records the SHA-256 of
# evidence.jsonl, so a regenerated file can be verified byte-for-byte against a
# committed report's numbers:
#
#   python -m ml.gpo_ocr_audit
#   (Get-FileHash reports/ocr_audit/evidence.jsonl -Algorithm SHA256)
#
reports/ocr_audit/.cache/
reports/ocr_audit/evidence.jsonl
reports/ocr_audit/contact_sheets/
reports/_smoke/
